+# Pieces · Summary card
+
+The card is cut along the seams the code already has: the frame (`src/card.mjs`),
+the header (`src/header.mjs`) and the trend (`src/trend.mjs`). The trend is cut in
+two because its emphasis on the best month can only be judged once the bars it
+stands out from are right. Build and judge the pieces in this order. Each piece
+is judged on its own, against its cut of the reference, once the pieces before it
+have passed.
+
+Rules for every piece:
+
+- The owner works from BRIEF.md, `data/summary.json` and the acceptance below.
+  Every value shown must be exact to the data.
+- Change only the files in the piece's owner scope. Do not change what an
+  earlier, ticked piece produced, except where this piece's scope says so.
+- Before reporting, run `npm test` (it must pass) and `npm run capture` (it must
+  write `captures/card.svg` and `captures/CAPTURE.md` for the current revision).
+- The reference cut points the critic at parts of the held-out reference. Those
+  parts are named here and never copied. The owner does not read the reference.
+- The critic ticks the piece's box on a pass, and only that box.
+
+---
+
+## Piece 1 · Card frame
+
+**Owner scope.** `src/card.mjs`. A new `src/layout.mjs` may be created to hold
+shared card geometry (card size, the outer margin, the inner width between the
+margins) for the header and trend to use later. `tests/card.test.mjs` may gain
+assertions, but no existing assertion may be removed or weakened. Do not touch
+`src/header.mjs` or `src/trend.mjs` in this piece.
+
+**Reference cut.** The single top-level element of the reference, the card
+itself, which sits outside both groups (first element row in
+REFERENCE-CAPTURE.md). Also the card clause of point 10 in REFERENCE.md (white
+card, hairline rounded border, no shadow, no gradient), the card's overall
+size, its font family, and the order in which the header and trend groups are
+composed.
+
+**Acceptance.**
+
+- The card has a white background shape that covers the whole card at top level,
+  outside the `header` and `trend` groups. It has a hairline neutral-grey border
+  and gently rounded corners, and the stroke is not clipped at the card's edge.
+- No shadow, no gradient, no filter, no extra decoration.
+- The card keeps its declared size, its viewBox and a single sans-serif font
+  family. The `header` group comes before the `trend` group, as it does now.
+- One outer margin and the inner width it leaves are defined once (in
+  `src/layout.mjs` or exported from the frame) and generous enough that later
+  pieces can keep everything clear of the edge.
+- `npm test` passes and the capture shows the new top-level element first.
+
+- [x] Piece 1 · Card frame: critic ticks on a pass
+
+---
+
+## Piece 2 · Header: title, period and three stat tiles
+
+**Owner scope.** `src/header.mjs`. A new `src/format.mjs` may be created for the
+compact value and change formatting, which the trend will reuse. Read the shared
+geometry from Piece 1, but do not change it. In `tests/card.test.mjs`, the "every
+stat appears" check may be adjusted so it still holds when labels are displayed
+in a different letter case. It must still require every stat's label, and it may
+add checks that every compact value appears. Do not touch `src/trend.mjs` or the
+frame's output.
+
+**Reference cut.** Every element of the reference's `header` group in
+REFERENCE-CAPTURE.md: the title, the period line, and the three tiles, each with
+its background, label, value and change line. Points 1 to 5 of REFERENCE.md: the
+tiles' span and widths, the fixed baselines inside a tile, the change signed by
+colour and mark, the compact values, and the hierarchy above the tiles.
+
+**Acceptance.**
+
+- The title sits at the top left, on the margin, in a larger semi-bold dark
+  text. The period line sits under it, on the same margin, in smaller muted grey
+  text.
+- Below them is one row of three tiles, one per stat, in data order. Each tile
+  has a light neutral-grey rounded background. The row starts exactly at the
+  left margin and ends exactly at the right margin. All three tiles have equal
+  widths and equal heights, with a small equal gap between them, so the row
+  lines up with the trend's width below it.
+- Inside each tile, three lines sit on fixed baselines that are the same in every
+  tile:
+  - a small, muted, upper-case, semi-bold label
+  - a large bold dark value, the dominant text of the tile, on one baseline
+    shared by all three tiles
+  - a small change line under the value
+  All three lines are inset from the tile's left edge by the same padding, and
+  none of them overlaps another line or the tile's edge.
+- Values use the compact spoken form BRIEF.md gives: millions or thousands with a
+  suffix, and money with a currency sign. Never show raw integers, and never show
+  revenue in cents. Each value is derived from its `unit` in the data, not
+  hard-coded.
+- Each change line is signed by both colour and mark: an up mark in one positive
+  colour for a rise, a down mark in one negative colour for a fall. Then the
+  absolute percentage to one decimal place, then a short phrase naming the
+  comparison with the previous period. A fall must read as a fall at a glance.
+  Text other than the change lines uses neutral greys only.
+- `npm test` passes, and the frame from Piece 1 is unchanged in the capture.
+
+- [x] Piece 2 · Header: critic ticks on a pass
+
+---
+
+## Piece 3 · Trend: section label, baseline, bars and month names
+
+**Owner scope.** `src/trend.mjs`. Read the shared geometry from Piece 1, and the
+formatting from Piece 2 if needed, but do not change their outputs. Do not touch
+`src/header.mjs`. Do not single out any month in this piece: that is Piece 4.
+
+**Reference cut.** In the reference's `trend` group in REFERENCE-CAPTURE.md, the
+section label, the baseline line, the twelve bars (their span, widths, gaps,
+corner rounding and common accent fill) and the twelve month names. Leave out
+the best month's distinct shade and its value callout. Points 6, 7 and 9 of
+REFERENCE.md apply, plus the data-colour clause of point 10 (one accent colour,
+no legend, no gridlines).
+
+**Acceptance.**
+
+- A small, muted, upper-case, semi-bold section label names what the bars show.
+  It sits on the left margin above the bars, in the same style as the tile
+  labels, with clear space between it and the tiles above.
+- One light neutral-grey baseline line runs from the left margin to the right
+  margin. All bars sit on it.
+- There are twelve bars, one per month, in data order. All have equal widths and
+  together fill the inner width, the first starting at the left margin and the
+  last ending at the right margin. A clear equal gap, a fraction of a bar's
+  width, separates neighbouring bars, so no two bars touch. The tops are
+  slightly rounded.
+- Bar heights are proportional to the data, with the tallest month reaching a
+  fixed maximum height. Leave room above the tallest bar for a callout in
+  Piece 4, below the section label.
+- Every bar uses one single, saturated accent colour, the only data colour on
+  the card. No legend, gradient, gridline or axis numbers.
+- Each month's name is centred under its bar in small muted grey text, below the
+  baseline and clear of the card's bottom edge.
+- `npm test` passes, and the frame and header are unchanged in the capture.
+
+- [ ] Piece 3 · Trend bars: critic ticks on a pass
+
+---
+
+## Piece 4 · Trend: the best month called out
+
+**Owner scope.** `src/trend.mjs`, plus additions to `src/format.mjs` if the
+callout needs a formatting variant. Any change to `src/format.mjs` must leave the
+header's output byte-for-byte unchanged. Do not touch anything else.
+
+**Reference cut.** In the reference's `trend` group in REFERENCE-CAPTURE.md, the
+best month's bar (its distinct shade) and the value text above it. This is point
+8 of REFERENCE.md, judged together with the one-accent rule of point 10.
+
+**Acceptance.**
+
+- The month with the highest revenue in the data is found from the data, not
+  hard-coded. Its bar is a darker shade of the same accent colour, and every
+  other bar keeps the plain accent colour. No second hue is introduced.
+- That month's revenue is shown above its bar, centred on the bar, in a compact
+  spoken money form consistent with the tile values. The text is small, dark and
+  semi-bold. It must not touch the bar, the section label or the header.
+- The value shown is exact to the data, at the precision the compact form
+  states.
+- `npm test` passes, and everything from Pieces 1 to 3 is unchanged in the
+  capture apart from the best month's fill and the added callout.
+
+- [ ] Piece 4 · Best month callout: critic ticks on a pass
